11	On 2026-11-10 Merill approved and asked to build "setting-bound parameters" in one go before 3.0 ships, so that later changes don't break anyone. This is PR 2758, branch feat/parameter-settings, worktree /Users/merill/github/maester-params.
12	
13	**What changed**
14	- Binding: `[MaesterParameter(Setting = 'X')]` binds a test parameter to a setting. Precedence is -Parameter, then the TestSettings row, then GlobalSettings, then the default. The result row shows Source = 'Setting'.
15	- Emergency access: EmergencyAccessAccounts is bound as `object[]` on its 7 tests. The helpers take the accounts as an argument.
16	- Defender for Endpoint: the MDE PolicyFiltering and ComplianceLogic parameters are bound to DefenderEndpoint* settings.
17	- MT.1195 lost the GsaQuickAccess... global setting.
18	- ParkedDomains is registered so that PR 2673 can bind it after 2758 lands.
19	
20	**Naming convention**
21	- Use Maximum/Minimum, then what is measured, then the unit, with a plain suffix (no `In`).
22	- Lists take plural names.
23	- Never use the names of PowerShell common parameters.
24	- About 20 parameters were renamed.
25	- `TestParameterNames.Tests.ps1` checks the mechanical rules.
26	- The attribute review (`Invoke-MtAttributeReview.ps1`) now also reviews `Parameters`, against the "Parameter names" section of attribute-rubric.md.
27	
28	**Why:** a test that read a setting with Get-MtSetting hid the value from Get-MtTest and from the results, and no test could override it. Matching parameters by name alone is unsafe: MaximumLockoutThreshold exists in both ASD and CIS, and each needs a different value.
29	
30	**How to apply:**
31	- New checks take shared values as bound parameters.
32	- Don't bind a value that a benchmark mandates.
33	- Follow the naming rules; see [[map-benchmarks-to-existing-tests]].
34	- "Luna model" in Merill's request was unclear. The review kept the existing review model (claude-opus-5-5).
